---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LevelCalculator from '../../components/LevelCalculator.tsx';

const title = 'SPL & amplifier';
const description =
  'Home cinema SPL and amplifier calculator: can your speakers and amplifier reach Dolby’s calibration level? Power needed, maximum level and headroom for every speaker at the main seat.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How much amplifier power does a home cinema need?',
    a: 'It depends on how sensitive your speakers are and how far away you sit. For 3 dB more you need twice the watts, and twice the distance costs 6 dB. The calculator shows for every speaker how many watts it needs for the loudest moments of a film at your seat.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does 2.83 V / 1 m mean for sensitivity?',
    a: 'Many manufacturers measure sensitivity with 2.83 volts instead of 1 watt. For an 8-ohm speaker that’s about the same. For a 4-ohm speaker, though, 2.83 volts is already 2 watts – so on paper it looks 3 dB louder than it would with 1 watt. The calculator converts this correctly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What level should I aim for?',
    a: 'For home cinemas, Dolby recommends setting each speaker at the main seat to 79 to 82 dB with a test noise, depending on room size. The loudest moments in a film are then 20 dB louder.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebApplication',
    name: 'Plan My Cinema – SPL & amplifier',
    url: 'https://plan-my-cinema.com/en/spl-amplifier/',
    applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication',
    operatingSystem: 'Web',
    inLanguage: 'en',
    offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'EUR' },
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
  },
];
---
<Base lang="en" page="levels" title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>SPL <span>&amp;</span> amplifier</h1>
    <p>Will your speakers and amplifier get loud enough for a real cinema feeling? Speaker positions and your seat come from the speaker planner.</p>
  </div>

  <LevelCalculator client:load lang="en" />

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>What is this about?</h2>
    <p>
      In a cinema, explosions can get really loud. For that to work at home too, your speakers and amplifier need enough power. This calculator
      checks for every speaker whether it can play loud enough at your seat – or whether it hits its limit.
    </p>

    <h3>How loud is “cinema loud”?</h3>
    <p>
      Loudness is measured in <strong>decibels (dB)</strong>. Dolby recommends setting each speaker to 79 to 82 dB with a test noise – depending on how
      big the room is. The loudest moments in a film are another 20 dB louder, so around 100 dB. Every speaker should be able to play that loud at
      your seat.
    </p>

    <h3>What makes a speaker loud?</h3>
    <p>
      Three things count. First, the speaker’s <strong>sensitivity</strong>: it’s on the data sheet and tells you how loud it already is with little
      power. Second, the amplifier’s <strong>power</strong> in watts. Third, the <strong>distance</strong> to your seat. The rule is: for 3 dB more you
      need twice the watts, and sitting twice as far away costs 6 dB. That’s why a high sensitivity often matters more than a huge amplifier.
    </p>

    <h3>And the subwoofer?</h3>
    <p>
      Films have their own channel for the deep bass. It is usually set 10 dB louder than the other speakers – a rule of thumb that you fine-tune
      later with a measurement. How many subwoofers you have and where they stand comes from the room modes calculator. Two subwoofers together are
      clearly louder than one.
    </p>

    <h3>How exact is this?</h3>
    <p>
      The calculator pretends there are no walls. A real room is usually a bit louder, especially in the bass, so the result is on the cautious side.
      It doesn’t take into account how much power your speakers can handle – that’s on the data sheet.
    </p>

    <details class="tech">
      <summary>For the technically minded: how we calculate</summary>

    <h3>Target level</h3>
    <p>
      Dolby’s <a href="https://www.dolby.com/siteassets/technologies/dolby-atmos/atmos-installation-guidelines-121318_r3.1.pdf" rel="noopener">Dolby Atmos Home Theater Installation Guidelines</a>{' '}
      (December 2018, p. 15) recommend calibrating every speaker to 79–82 dB SPL (C-weighted) at the main seat, depending on room size, using pink noise
      at −20 dBFS RMS. At full scale (0 dBFS) the level is 20 dB higher – 102 dB for an 82 dB calibration. The calculator checks whether each speaker reaches that
      level at the main seat.
    </p>
    <h3>Level and power</h3>
    <p><code>Level = sensitivity + 10 · log10(P / P_ref) − 20 · log10(distance / 1 m)</code></p>
    <p>
      P_ref is 1 W for a 1 W / 1 m rating and 2.83² / impedance for a 2.83 V / 1 m rating. Twice the power adds 3 dB; twice the distance costs 6 dB.
      From this the calculator derives the power needed for the target and the maximum level your amplifier delivers, using distances from the speaker
      planner.
    </p>
    <h3>Subwoofers and LFE</h3>
    <p>
      The LFE channel is usually played louder than the main channels. The calculator assumes +10 dB – a rule of thumb, not a standard, to be
      fine-tuned by measuring in the room. Number and positions of the subs come from the room modes calculator. Several subs are summed in phase
      (twice as many ≈ +6 dB); with a DBA only the front array counts. No room gain is included: in a real room the bass is usually much louder, so the
      result is conservative.
    </p>
    <h3>Limits</h3>
    <p>
      The calculation assumes a free field without reflections from walls and ceiling – real rooms are usually a little louder, so the result errs on
      the safe side. It ignores speaker power handling, power compression at high levels, several channels playing together. Calling less than 3 dB of headroom “tight” is our own choice.
    </p>
    </details>

    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <details>
        <summary>{f.q}</summary>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </article>
</Base>
